// The `prelude.string` sub-module: operations over `string` beyond concatenation (`++` /
// templates). Called qualified — `string.length`, `string.split` — via the default import. Indices
// are Unicode code points, not UTF-16 units, so a character outside the BMP counts once.

@"The number of Unicode code points."
primitive agent length(value: string) -> integer

@"The substrings between occurrences of @separator@. An empty separator splits into single
code points."
primitive agent split(value: string, separator: string) -> array[string]

@"The parts joined with @separator@ between them."
primitive agent join(parts: array[string], separator: string) -> string

@"The code points from @start@ (inclusive) to @end@ (exclusive), 0-based, clamped to the bounds."
primitive agent slice(value: string, start: integer, end: integer) -> string

@"Whether @search@ occurs in the string."
primitive agent contains(value: string, search: string) -> boolean

@"Whether the string starts with @search@."
primitive agent starts_with(value: string, search: string) -> boolean

@"Whether the string ends with @search@."
primitive agent ends_with(value: string, search: string) -> boolean

@"The code-point index of the first occurrence of @search@, or `null` when it does not occur."
primitive agent index_of(value: string, search: string) -> integer | null

@"The string with every occurrence of @search@ replaced by @replacement@ (literal text, not a
pattern). An empty @search@ returns the value unchanged."
primitive agent replace(value: string, search: string, replacement: string) -> string

@"The string with leading and trailing whitespace removed."
primitive agent trim(value: string) -> string

@"CUT the string to at most @cap@ code points, and SAY SO when it cut: a value that fits comes back
untouched, and one that does not comes back as its head plus @marker@ — the whole result still at most
@cap@ points, because the marker's own length is taken out of the head first.

This is the one bound every rendered text takes on its way to somewhere with a limit: a chat platform's
per-message ceiling, a tool result a model reads, a summary a store cell holds. The composition
(`slice(value, 0, cap - length(marker)) ++ marker`) is short but it is not SAFE — the arithmetic is
where it goes wrong, and always in the same two ways. Subtracting the marker AFTER slicing overshoots
the cap by exactly the marker (the platform rejects the message, in production, on the one long input
nobody tested), and a @cap@ smaller than the marker makes the slice bound negative, so the truncation
marker alone is the whole answer and it is still too long. Both are silent until the day they are not,
which is what makes this a canonical spelling rather than a shorthand: the judgement being encoded is
`cap` MEANS the whole answer.

A @marker@ that alone exceeds @cap@ is itself cut to @cap@ — the answer is then all marker, which is
the honest reading of a budget too small to say anything in. An empty @marker@ is a plain hard cut, for
a sink whose truncation the reader can already see."
agent fit(
  @"The text to bound." value: string,
  @"The ceiling, in code points; the ANSWER never exceeds it (the marker is inside the budget, not added to it)." cap: integer,
  @"What a cut answer ends with, e.g. \"…\" or \" [truncated]\"; empty for a bare hard cut." marker: string,
) -> string {
  if (length(value = value) <= cap) {
    value
  } else {
    // The marker is paid for out of the budget, BEFORE the head is taken — the whole point of the agent.
    // A marker that does not fit the budget at all leaves no head to keep, so the answer is the marker cut
    // to the cap (which also covers a zero or negative @cap@: `slice` clamps).
    if (length(value = marker) >= cap) {
      slice(value = marker, start = 0, end = cap)
    } else {
      f"${slice(value = value, start = 0, end = cap - length(value = marker))}${marker}"
    }
  }
}

@"The string uppercased (Unicode default casing, no locale)."
primitive agent to_upper(value: string) -> string

@"The string lowercased (Unicode default casing, no locale)."
primitive agent to_lower(value: string) -> string

@"Render a scalar as its string form (an integer prints without a decimal point). For a composite
value use `json.stringify(value = ...)` instead."
primitive agent to_string(value: null | boolean | number | string) -> string

@"Read the string as a base-10 integer (an optional sign and digits, nothing else), or `null` when
it is not one — including a magnitude the number model cannot hold exactly. Compose with `trim` for
padded input; for a diagnosed parse use `json.validate[integer](value = json.parse(text = ...))`
instead."
primitive agent to_integer(value: string) -> integer | null

@"Read the string as a number (any JSON-style numeric form), or `null` when it is not one. Compose
with `trim` for padded input; for a diagnosed parse use
`json.validate[number](value = json.parse(text = ...))` instead."
primitive agent to_number(value: string) -> number | null

@"The string left-padded with repetitions of @padding@ until it is @width@ code points long — the
zero-pad of a date field, the column alignment of a table row. A value already @width@ or longer is
returned unchanged (never truncated); the last repetition of a multi-point @padding@ is cut to fit.
An empty @padding@ returns the value unchanged."
primitive agent pad_start(value: string, width: integer, padding: string) -> string

// ── byte-level text codecs ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// base64 and percent-encoding are the wire encodings a REST API asks for (an email's `raw` body, a
// query parameter); Katari has no byte / code-point access, so they cannot be composed from the other
// string agents and must be primitives. Each operates on TEXT: it encodes the string's UTF-8 bytes,
// and decodes back to a UTF-8 string (a payload that is not UTF-8 text decodes best-effort). The url
// (`-_`) alphabet is the one Gmail / JWT / many Google APIs use.

@"The Base64 (standard `+/` alphabet, padded) of the string's UTF-8 bytes — the wire form a REST body
or an HTTP Basic header takes. `from_base64` is the inverse for UTF-8-text payloads."
primitive agent to_base64(value: string) -> string

@"Decode standard Base64 (`+/`, padding optional) back to a UTF-8 string — the inverse of
`to_base64` for text payloads. Non-Base64 characters are ignored and a non-UTF-8 payload decodes
best-effort, so this is total (never throws); use `files.from_base64` when the bytes are a binary
file, not text."
primitive agent from_base64(value: string) -> string

@"The Base64URL (`-_` alphabet, unpadded) of the string's UTF-8 bytes — the form Gmail's message
`raw`, a JWT segment, and many Google APIs take. `from_base64url` is the inverse for text payloads."
primitive agent to_base64url(value: string) -> string

@"Decode Base64URL (`-_`, padding optional) back to a UTF-8 string — the inverse of `to_base64url`
for text payloads (a Gmail message body's `data`). Total: unknown characters are ignored and a
non-UTF-8 payload decodes best-effort."
primitive agent from_base64url(value: string) -> string

@"Percent-encode the string for use inside ONE URL component — a query value, a path segment. Every
character outside the unreserved set (`A-Z a-z 0-9 - _ . ~`) becomes `%HH` of its UTF-8 bytes (a
space is `%20`, not `+`). `url_decode` is the inverse."
primitive agent url_encode(value: string) -> string

@"Decode a percent-encoded (`%HH`) string back to its text — the inverse of `url_encode`. A `+` is
left as-is (it is a literal plus in a path; a form body's `+`-for-space is a different convention the
caller handles). A malformed `%` escape is left verbatim, so this is total."
primitive agent url_decode(value: string) -> string
